# This repository uses mdse

This repository's engineering project is represented as a **Project model**: a set of
strongly typed JSON instances under `.mdse/`, conforming to the mdse meta-model.
Prefer reading the model over re-deriving context from source.
Use `mdse context .mdse` (or `--work-item <slug>`) for a lean initial view,
then open exact typed instances when an operation needs their full content.

## Before answering questions about this project

1. Read `.mdse/model/overview.mdse-overview` — what this repository is.
2. Read `.mdse/model/architecture.mdse-architecture` — how it is built.
3. For a specific work item, read `.mdse/work-items/<slug>/workitem.mdse-workitem` for its
   `status`, then its `requirements/`, `design/`, `decisions/`, `tasks/` as needed.

Every instance's `status` field tells you where it is in its lifecycle — do not infer
progress from file existence alone.

**Load the model before proposing anything new.** This project's model persists and
accumulates — new work extends it, it does not start over. Before proposing a work item,
requirement or design, check what the model already says: existing work items
(`Project.workItems`), accepted decisions, active constraints, and `Product.nonGoals`.
Extend what exists rather than duplicating it, and if a proposal contradicts something
already recorded, say so and ask rather than writing over it silently.

An instance marked `"provenance": "onboarded"` was reconstructed from this codebase by
`/mdse-onboard`, not specified by a person — treat it as a reading of the code to verify,
not as settled intent.

## Before writing or editing code

If the change relates to an existing work item, read that work item's `design.mdse-design`
and the relevant `Task` instances first — they carry the contracts, conventions and
constraints the implementation must respect (`.mdse/work-items/<slug>/constraints/`).

All development in this repository MUST go through the mdse workflow — do not freehand
it: `requirements → analysis → design → taskify → implement → verify → reconcile`.

The only exception is a small, well-scoped edit to an existing work item — a bug fix, a
copy change, a one-file tweak that changes no contract, requirement or architecture
decision. That may be made directly, following the conventions already recorded for the
relevant workspace at `.mdse/model/workspaces/`, without the full workflow. When in
doubt whether an edit qualifies, it doesn't — run it through the workflow.

Picking work back up after a break (pending or in-progress tasks left over from an
earlier session)? Run `/mdse-resume` rather than manually finding and re-running
`/mdse-implement` for each one — it drives every leftover task through to verify and
reconcile in one pass.

## After writing or editing code

- Do not hand-edit files under `.mdse/` into an invalid shape.
- If you changed something a `Task` or `Requirement` describes, update that instance's
  `status` and, where one exists, record the change via an `Execution` instance rather
  than leaving the model silently out of date.
- Validate before considering the change complete: `mdse validate .mdse`.

## Rules

- SHALL NOT freehand development beyond a small, well-scoped edit (see above) — run it
  through the mdse workflow instead of writing code or specs ad hoc.
- SHALL NOT write prose documents into `.mdse/` — it holds typed instances only.
- SHALL NOT delete an instance directly — set its lifecycle status, or use the
  reviewed `/mdse-compact` workflow so the exact original enters cold history.
- SHALL NOT invent fields outside an instance's schema.
